<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Elea</h1><p class="st-meta">540 BCE – 273 BCE (approx.) · capital Elea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city on the Cilento coast founded by refugees from Phocaea, home of the philosophers Parmenides and Zeno and of the Eleatic school.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 540 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaean Greeks</a>, who had fled their city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and then lost much of their fleet at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> off <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corsican-republic">Corsica</a>, founded Elea (later Roman Velia) on a headland south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/poseidonia">Poseidonia</a> with help from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhegion">Rhegion</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Elea lived by the sea: fishing, trade and shipping rather than farming, as its hilly territory was poor. It is best known for philosophy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a>, said to have written the city's laws, and his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-elea">Zeno</a> founded the Eleatic school, which shaped Greek thinking about being and change. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porta-rosa">Porta Rosa</a>, a 4th-century BCE arched gateway, is an early true arch in a Greek city. Elea resisted Lucanian pressure while other Greek cities fell.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Elea allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the early 3rd century BCE and supplied ships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>; Rome recruited priestesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a> from Velia. It kept Greek speech and became a Roman municipium after 89 BCE. Silting of its harbour led to decline, and its remains are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 540 BCE – 273 